cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or the archived results, please cite the paper."
title: "Localizing and repairing the in-context cliff: a complementary learning systems account of fast and slow learning in large language models"
abstract: >-
  Many-shot in-context learning is not monotone: for some models accuracy collapses once
  demonstrations exceed a threshold -- the in-context cliff. Vulnerability is set by the
  post-training recipe rather than by scale or architecture, and the failure localizes
  causally to transformer blocks 8-15 by three independent methods (function-vector
  causal tracing, induction-head scoring, and targeted LoRA consolidation): consolidation
  restricted to that band repairs the cliff, while the same intervention at the late
  readout does not. Framed as a complementary learning systems account, with in-context
  learning as the hippocampal fast path and fine-tuning as neocortical consolidation.
authors:
